which is a linear function plus a positively homogeneous even function.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary} \label{cor:onlyodd-lin}
The only odd function that bias-free two-layer (leaky) ReLU networks can express is the linear function.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Deep Bias-Free (Leaky) ReLU Networks} \label{sec:deep-express}
Similarly to two-layer bias-free ReLU networks, deep bias-free ReLU networks can express only positively homogeneous functions. However, in contrast to two-layer bias-free ReLU networks, deep bias-free ReLU networks can express some odd nonlinear functions.
For instance, for two-dimensional input $\vx=[x_1,x_2]^\T$, the function below is odd, nonlinear, and can be implemented by a three-layer bias-free ReLU network,
\begin{align}
g(\vx) = \sigma(\sigma(x_1)-\sigma(x_2)) - \sigma(\sigma(-x_1)-\sigma(-x_2))
,\quad \text{where } \sigma(z) = \max(z,0).
\end{align}
Thus, we have a depth separation result for bias-free ReLU networks: there exist odd nonlinear functions, such as $g(\vx)$ above, that two-layer bias-free ReLU networks cannot express but deep bias-free ReLU networks can. 


\section{Learning Dynamics}

\subsection{Two-Layer Bias-Free (Leaky) ReLU Networks} \label{sec:L2-dynamics-sym}
\cref{sec:L2-express} has proved that two-layer bias-free (Leaky) ReLU networks cannot express odd functions except linear functions. We now show that under the \conditionref{ass:sym-data} on the dataset, two-layer bias-free (Leaky) ReLU networks not only find a linear solution but also have the same learning dynamics as a two-layer linear network.

\begin{condition} \label{ass:sym-data}
The dataset satisfies the following two symmetry conditions:
\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt,topsep=2pt]
\item The empirical input data distribution is even: $p(\vx) = p(-\vx)$.
\item The target model is odd: $y(\vx) = - y(-\vx)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{condition}

Under \conditionref{ass:sym-data}, the dynamics of two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU networks and linear networks initialized with small weights can both be approximated by a linear differential equation in the early phase of learning. Thus weights in the ReLU network form the same rank-one structure as weights in a linear network, as specified in \cref{thm:early}. At the end of the early phase, the weights are rank-one with small errors. To simplify the analysis, \assumptionref{ass:L2-rank1} assumes the weights are exactly rank-one, which is justified by the initialization approaching $0$ and the width approaching infinity. Simulations in \cref{fig:L2-sym-error} also show that errors are indeed small in the finite case.
\begin{assumption} \label{ass:L2-rank1}
The two-layer bias-free network's weights satisfy $\mW_1 = \mW_2^\T \vr^\T$ where $\vr$ is an arbitrary unit vector.
The second-layer weights $\mW_2$ have equal L2 norms for their positive and negative elements. 
\end{assumption}

Under \conditionref{ass:sym-data} and \assumptionref{ass:L2-rank1}, the learning dynamics of two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU networks reduces to \cref{eq:L2-relu-reduced}, which is the same as that of linear networks given in \cref{eq:lin-ode} modulo scale factors. Thus, apart from the fact that learning is $(\alpha+1)/2$ times slower and the weights are $\sqrt{2/(\alpha+1)}$ times larger, the ReLU network has the same learning dynamics as its linear counterpart. We formally state this equivalence below.
\begin{theorem} \label{thm:late-exact}
A two-layer (leaky) ReLU network and a linear network are trained with square or logistic loss starting from weights which differ by a scale factor, $\mW(0)=\sqrt{2/(\alpha+1)} \, \mW^\lin(0)$. Under \conditionref{ass:sym-data} on the dataset and \assumptionref{ass:L2-rank1} on the weights at time $t=0$, we have that $\forall \, t \geq 0$, \assumptionref{ass:L2-rank1} retains valid and:
\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt,topsep=2pt]
\item The (leaky) ReLU network implements the same linear function as the linear network with scaled time
\begin{align} \label{eq:f=f_lin}
f(\vx; \mW(t)) = f^\lin \left( \vx; \mW^\lin \left( \frac{\alpha+1}2 t \right) \right) .
\end{align}
\item The weights in the (leaky) ReLU network are the same as scaled weights in the linear network
\begin{align} \label{eq:W=W_lin}
\mW(t) = \sqrt {\frac2{\alpha+1}} \mW^\lin \left( \frac{\alpha+1}2 t \right) .
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
We validate \cref{thm:late-exact} and the plausibility of \assumptionref{ass:L2-rank1} with numerical simulations in \cref{fig:L2-dynamics}.

