If the input covariance is white, we can further write down the exact time-course solution in closed form for two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU networks by adopting the solutions of linear networks \cite[Theorem~3.1]{clem22prior}, as specified in \corollaryref{cor:exact-sol}.

Since the time evolution of two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU networks is the same as that of linear networks (modulo scale factors), their converged weights will also be the same. For learning with square loss, linear networks converge to the ordinary least squares solution \citep{saxe13exact}. For linearly separable binary classification with logistic loss, linear networks converge to the max-margin solution \citep{soudry18linsep}. Thus two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU networks also converge to these solutions when they behave like linear networks; see \cref{supp:global-min}. 
\begin{corollary} \label{cor:L2-converge}
Under the same conditions as \cref{thm:late-exact}, the two-layer bias-free (leaky) ReLU network converges to a linear solution $f(\vx; \mW(\infty)) = {\vw^*}^\T \vx$. For square loss, $\vw^*$ is the ordinary least squares solution, $\mSigma^{-1} \vbeta$, which is the global minimum. For linearly separable binary classification with logistic loss, $\vw^*$ is the max-margin (hard margin SVM) solution.
\end{corollary}


\subsection{Deep Bias-Free ReLU Networks} \label{sec:deep-dynamics}
The equivalence between two-layer ReLU and linear networks stated in \cref{thm:late-exact} does not extend to deep ReLU networks. Nonetheless, we find deep bias-free ReLU networks can form low-rank weights similar to those in deep linear networks when the empirical input distribution is even, the target function is linear, and the initialization is small. 
For the first and last layers, the linear and ReLU networks form rank-one weights of the same structure. For intermediate layers, weights of the linear network are rank-one, while weights of the ReLU network are rank-two and all weights are approximately non-negative as shown in \cref{fig:deep-lowrank}. Deep ReLU networks that have formed such low-rank weights will retain the low-rank structure and implement a linear map; see \cref{supp:deep}.
